WEBVTT

00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.877
The world's tallest
building isn't anchored to

00:00:02.887 --> 00:00:04.199
solid bedrock.

00:00:04.209 --> 00:00:08.137
The Burj Khalifa, 828 metres tall, stands

00:00:08.147 --> 00:00:10.887
on weak sand and soft sandstone.

00:00:10.897 --> 00:00:13.574
So engineers poured a concrete slab about

00:00:13.584 --> 00:00:16.950
3.7 metres thick, called a raft.

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:18.988
Under it are nearly two hundred concrete

00:00:18.998 --> 00:00:21.450
piles, each a metre and a half

00:00:21.460 --> 00:00:22.269
wide.

00:00:22.279 --> 00:00:24.848
Together, the raft and piles spread the

00:00:24.858 --> 00:00:27.153
tower's weight down into the ground.

00:00:27.163 --> 00:00:29.126
And because the groundwater is full of

00:00:29.136 --> 00:00:33.211
salts, the foundation is
protected against corrosion.

00:00:33.221 --> 00:00:35.419
The tallest tower on Earth, held up

00:00:35.429 --> 00:00:37.807
by spreading its weight, not by solid

00:00:37.817 --> 00:00:38.804
bedrock.
